<interrogationes unitas="internuntius">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I expose a C function that page JavaScript can call?
    <responsum symbolum="internuntius_creare internuntius_praebere InternuntiusTractator internuntius_tractare">Create
      the bridge once with internuntius_creare(piscina, missor, datum),
      register each method with internuntius_praebere(inx, "titulus",
      tractator, datum), and feed every incoming message to
      internuntius_tractare(inx, nuntium, piscina_vocationis). The
      message is JSON {"id": 7, "methodus": "...", "argumenta": {...}};
      the reply {"id": 7, "fructus": ...} or {"id": 7, "culpa":
      {"nuntius": "..."}} goes out through the missor.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how does a handler report an error instead of a result?
    <responsum symbolum="InternuntiusTractator internuntius_tractare">Set
      *culpa_exitus to a non-empty chorda and return NIHIL (the return
      value is ignored once culpa has length > 0); the caller gets
      {"culpa": {"nuntius": ...}} and the culpae counter goes up.
      Returning NIHIL with culpa left empty is a SUCCESS whose fructus is
      JSON null. argumenta is NIHIL when the message had no "argumenta"
      key, so the handler must check it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the piscina_vocationis and what lives in it?
    <responsum symbolum="internuntius_tractare internuntius_eventum_mittere internuntius_creare">The
      per-call arena: the parsed message tree, everything the handler
      builds for its result, and the serialized reply all live there,
      and the caller marks and resets it. The long-lived piscina given
      to internuntius_creare holds only the bridge and its method
      registry (method names are copied into it). Allocate the
      handler's result in the piscina argument it receives, never in a
      static.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can my missor keep the textus it is given?
    <responsum symbolum="InternuntiusMissor chorda_transcribere vitrea_missor" via="lib/vitrea_servus.c lib/vitrea_macos.m">Only
      by copying it. The text lives in the per-call piscina and is valid
      only during the missor call; it is a chorda (length + bytes, not
      NUL-terminated). lib/vitrea_servus.c copies it with
      chorda_transcribere into its request piscina; the WebKit missor
      (vitrea_missor in lib/vitrea_macos.m) escapes and evaluates it at
      once.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I register the same method name twice, for example to override a handler?
    <responsum symbolum="internuntius_praebere">No:
      internuntius_praebere returns FALSUM for a duplicate name (and for
      an empty name or a NIHIL handler). The registry is meant as code
      written once, so a duplicate is treated as a wiring error. There
      is no unregister. Most callers cast the result to (vacuum), so a
      refused duplicate goes unnoticed; check it when names are
      computed.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can the request id be a string, as in JSON-RPC?
    <responsum symbolum="internuntius_tractare json_est_integer">No.
      Only a JSON integer id is recognized (json_est_integer). A string,
      float or null id is treated as no id: the call is still handled
      and a reply is still sent, but without "id", so the JS side cannot
      route it. Malformed JSON or a non-object message gets an error
      reply with no id.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I push an event from C to the page without a request?
    <responsum symbolum="internuntius_eventum_mittere">internuntius_eventum_mittere(inx,
      "titulus", datum, piscina_vocationis) sends {"eventus": titulus,
      "datum": datum} through the same missor (datum NIHIL becomes
      null) and counts it in eventus_missi.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the praebitor callback take an InternuntiusModus parameter?
    <responsum symbolum="InternuntiusPraebitor InternuntiusModus" via="lib/vitrea_servus.c apps/forum/forum.c">So
      that the same registration function serves both the local window
      (INTERNUNTIUS_MODUS_LOCALIS, full trust) and a socket transport
      (INTERNUNTIUS_MODUS_PUBLICUS, where test-harness methods must be
      left out). It is a parameter rather than a config field on
      purpose: the provider sees the mode in its signature and cannot
      forget to check it. lib/vitrea_servus.c calls it with
      INTERNUNTIUS_MODUS_PUBLICUS; apps/forum/forum.c is a provider that
      registers some methods only when modus is
      INTERNUNTIUS_MODUS_LOCALIS.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I put arbitrary text safely inside a JavaScript string literal?
    <responsum symbolum="internuntius_effugere_js vitrea_missor" via="lib/vitrea_macos.m lib/speculum.c">internuntius_effugere_js(textus,
      piscina) escapes backslash, double quote, control bytes (\n \r \t
      short, others \uXXXX), '&lt;' (so '&lt;/script' cannot close an
      inline script) and U+2028/U+2029, which are legal in JSON but not
      in a raw JS literal. It does not add the surrounding quotes; you
      write those yourself (vitrea_missor wraps the result in
      internuntius._accipere("...")). Returns an empty chorda if
      piscina is NIHIL.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I list the registered methods or see how many calls failed?
    <responsum symbolum="internuntius_methodi internuntius_fructus InternuntiusFructus">internuntius_methodi(inx,
      piscina) returns a new Xar of chorda (method names, in
      registration order; the names themselves stay in the bridge's
      piscina). internuntius_fructus(inx) returns the counters by value:
      vocationes_tractatae (successful dispatches), culpae (error
      replies sent, including malformed messages and unknown methods)
      and eventus_missi. A NIHIL inx gives all zeros.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
